<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import TopPages from './lib/TopPages.abide'
import topPagesSource from './lib/TopPages.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import rankPagesSource from './lib/rankPages.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import topPagesSpecSource from './tests/TopPages.e2e.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Name the share before changing the layer">
        <Example
            id="share-of-a-sort"
            title="`traceOp(page, op, body)` and `shares(trace, spec)` — one layer's part of a click"
            summary="Ranking five thousand pages takes a sort and a render. Its spec clicks the other ranking and asks what part of the click the sort was."
            files={{
                'lib/TopPages.abide': topPagesSource,
                'lib/rankPages.ts': rankPagesSource,
                'tests/TopPages.e2e.ts': topPagesSpecSource,
            }}
        >
            <TopPages />
        </Example>

        <p>Making a layer twice as fast saves at most the part of the op it was. Find that part
        first: if the sort is a tenth of the click, the best a faster sort can do is a tenth.</p>

        <p>The code under test marks its layer with <code>performance.measure</code>, and the spec
        marks the whole op the same way, from the click to the frame that shows it.
        <code>traceOp</code> records what Chromium did over that window, user timing included, and
        <code>shares</code> divides the one by the other.</p>

        <p><code>ceilingCpu</code> is the part of the processor's time the layer took.
        <code>ceilingObserved</code> is what a person could notice, and it is zero whenever the
        whole op fits in a frame, because the result appears at the same frame either way. A layer
        measured outside the op throws rather than reading as a smaller part.</p>

        <p>The spec runs in Chromium only, because the trace is read over the DevTools protocol, and
        it runs against the served site like any browser test.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What the engine did besides your code">
        <p>A <code>Trace</code> also counts what the engine did over the window: style
        recalculations, layouts, paints, and layouts forced by a read after a write. Two versions
        that take the same script time can still differ here, and one with a forced layout in a
        loop is the usual reason a list feels slow. The card's spec asserts the click forced
        none.</p>

        <p><code>measuresNamed</code> lists every measure of one name in the trace, and
        <code>durationOf</code> sums them.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/measure/price-code-against-the-hand-written-version')}>Ratios</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
